  "verbalization": "Mollicellin N appears to have multiple targets that ultimately lead to SBC-1 through a series of biochemical pathways. The evidence suggests that Mollicellin N interacts with Leukocyte common antigen, which is a target of 3Beta-Hydroxyolean-12-En-23,29-Dioic Acid, and this compound further targets SBC-1. Similarly, Mollicellin N also interacts with A704, which is a target of the same compound, and this pathway also leads to SBC-1. Additionally, Mollicellin N interacts with LC4-1, which is targeted by a different compound that also leads to SBC-1. These findings collectively indicate that Mollicellin N may act through multiple distinct pathways, each involving different intermediates, ultimately resulting in the inhibition of SBC-1. The integration of these pathways highlights the complexity of Mollicellin N's mechanism of action, suggesting that it may exert its effects through various molecular mechanisms that converge on SBC-1.",
